# Covariance Matrix Analysis

This notebook compares:
- Sample covariance matrix
- Shrinkage (Ledoit–Wolf) covariance matrix

The goal is to show why shrinkage produces more stable risk estimates
for portfolio optimization.

import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

from src.data_loader import load_price_data, compute_returns
from src.covariance import sample_covariance, shrinkage_covariance

tickers = ["RELIANCE.NS", "TCS.NS", "INFY.NS", "HDFCBANK.NS", "ICICIBANK.NS"]
start_date = "2019-01-01"
end_date = "2024-12-31"

prices = load_price_data(tickers, start_date, end_date)
returns = compute_returns(prices)

sample_cov = sample_covariance(returns)
shrink_cov = shrinkage_covariance(returns)

sample_cov, shrink_cov

pd.DataFrame({
    "Sample Variance": sample_cov.diagonal(),
    "Shrinkage Variance": shrink_cov.diagonal()
}, index=returns.columns)

plt.figure(figsize=(6,4))
plt.imshow(sample_cov, cmap="viridis")
plt.title("Sample Covariance Matrix")
plt.colorbar()
plt.xticks(range(len(returns.columns)), returns.columns, rotation=45)
plt.yticks(range(len(returns.columns)), returns.columns)
plt.show()

plt.figure(figsize=(6,4))
plt.imshow(shrink_cov, cmap="viridis")
plt.title("Shrinkage Covariance Matrix")
plt.colorbar()
plt.xticks(range(len(returns.columns)), returns.columns, rotation=45)
plt.yticks(range(len(returns.columns)), returns.columns)
plt.show()


## Key Insight

The sample covariance matrix is highly sensitive to noise in historical returns,
which leads to unstable portfolio weights during optimization.

Shrinkage covariance reduces estimation error by pulling extreme values toward
a structured target, resulting in more stable and realistic risk estimates.

This stability is critical for both Mean–Variance and Black–Litterman optimization.

